    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Mitsubishi Returns to Las Vegas With the Fresh 2028 Outlander Diamond Trail Edition\nVolkswagen Halts the Development of the ID. Golf. \"We Can't Afford It!\"\n6 photos\nPhoto: Volkswagen\nVolkswagen has officially suspended preliminary development work on its highly anticipated all-electric ID Golf at its headquarters in Wolfsburg. In an internal memo sent to employees earlier today, the automaker's CEO bluntly states: \"We simply can't afford this work right now!\"\nThomas Schafer, CEO of Volkswagen, informed employees about the halt of the project on Friday, October 30. The unexpected decision confirms the mounting financial distress that Europe's largest auto group has been going through. The auto giant is currently going through a massive structural overhaul.\nThe decision to freeze the project was driven by severe cost pressures at the flagship Wolfsburg plant, which has fallen short of its cost-cutting goals by a massive 300 million euros, which translates to $335 million.\nThe Volkswagen Group reported a 5.9 million euro ($6.6 million) operating loss for the third quarter. This marks Volkswagen's largest quarterly deficit in over a decade. The previous massive deficit dated way back to the 2015 Dieselgate emissions scandal.\nPhoto: Volkswagen The Volkswagen ID Golf , if it ever hits the market, is set to be underpinned by Volkswagen’s Scalable Systems Platform (SSP), designed to merge the existing architecture into a unified, highly efficient platform that would significantly lower production costs and help the group's models compete with low-cost Chinese rivals.\nHowever, the SSP architecture has faced multiple software hurdles and development delays, heavily complicating the ID Golf's timeline. The company’s works council insists this temporary spending freeze will not push back the model’s eventual market launch, but is well aware that the freeze is yet another sign of how strained the group’s financial situation actually is.\n\" We simply can't afford it, \" CEO Schafer told employees in the memo sent this morning, as reported by Automotive News. The halt comes amid a deeply hostile business environment. Volkswagen Group is currently moving forward with plans to cut up to 100,000 jobs and potentially close up to four factories across Germany.\nWhile factories in Emden and Zwickau are reportedly making progress to slash jobs, they are among the sites facing potential closure. Wolfsburg itself is safe from closure, but must drastically lean out its operations.\nPhoto: Volkswagen Tensions are inching closer to the boiling point with the powerful union IG Metall, which has been warning of widespread strikes starting December 1, 2026, if the employer and employees do not reach a resolution.\nIn the meantime, Volkswagen Group's premium brand, Audi, will be investing $4 billion into a new plant in the United States. Donald Trump officially announced the Georgia assembly plant at a campaign rally in Perry, Georgia. Audi CEO Gernot Doellner even joined him on stage, leading to massive backlash steered him and the company.\nAs the Golf EV goes on ice, Volkswagen is leaning heavily back on internal combustion engines to preserve cash flow. VW has temporarily halted or paused production of the ID. 4 and ID. Buzz for the U.S. market. In the meantime, the production of the gas-powered eighth-generation Golf is scheduled to relocate from Wolfsburg to Puebla, Mexico, in 2027.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolkswagen Volkswagen ID Golf Volkswagen Golf Volkswagen Group Volkswagen plant\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Snow-Bound Porsche Mission S Looks Official – But There’s a Catch",
